%[text] # result Class
%[text] **J.G. Vermond, JVsound**
%[text] Results of a loudspeaker system calculation.
%[text] **Namespace:** none (global namespace)
%[text] **Superclasses:** none
%%
%[text] ## Description
%[text] A `result` object holds the arrays that [`lspsys`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','lspsys','lspsysdoc.m'))) calculates over `Frequency`: the source voltage and current, the volume velocity of the diaphragm and the volume velocity of the radiated sound. The electrical impedance, the sound pressure, the sound pressure level and the excursion of the diaphragm are derived from these arrays when they are read, so they always follow the distance `MicRadius`, the only property that can be changed.
%[text] The symbols in the equations are listed in [`symbols`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','symbols.m'))).
%%
%[text] ## Creation
%[text] A `result` object is created by the method [`createResult`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','lspsys','methods','createresultdoc','createresultdoc.m'))) of `lspsys`, which solves the system and fills the arrays. `obj = result` creates an object with all arrays set to zero; only `lspsys` can set them.
%%
%[text] ## Properties
%[text] The properties are listed in the order of the class definition file.
%%
%[text] ### `Frequency` — Frequencies of the calculation
%[text] `(1,:) double` \[Hz\]
%[text] Frequencies $ f $ at which the system was calculated, copied from `lspsys.Frequency`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = ?lspsys`
%%
%[text] ### `SourceVoltage` — Voltage of the source
%[text] `(1,:) double` \[V\]
%[text] RMS voltage $ e\_g $ of the source at every frequency, from `lspsys.SourceVoltage`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = ?lspsys`
%%
%[text] ### `SourceCurrent` — Current of the source
%[text] `(1,:) double` \[A\]
%[text] Complex current $ i\_g $ that the source delivers to the voice coil.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = ?lspsys`
%%
%[text] ### `DiaphragmVolumeVelocity` — Volume velocity of the diaphragm
%[text] `(1,:) double` \[m3/s\]
%[text] Complex volume velocity $ U\_d $ of the diaphragm: its velocity times its area $ S\_d $.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = ?lspsys`
%%
%[text] ### `RadiatedVolumeVelocity` — Volume velocity of the radiated sound
%[text] `(1,:) double` \[m3/s\]
%[text] Complex volume velocity $ U\_{rad} $ that the enclosure radiates into the environment, from the acoustical side of the network (see [`solve2PortNetwork`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','lspsys','methods','solve2portnetworkdoc','solve2portnetworkdoc.m')))). For a closed box it is the volume velocity of the diaphragm that reaches the front.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = ?lspsys`
%%
%[text] ### `RadiationAngle` — Radiation angle
%[text] `(1,1) string`
%[text] Radiation angle of the enclosure, copied from `lspsys.RadiationAngle`. It sets the solid angle $ \\Omega $ in `Pressure`. Currently only `"2pi"` is supported: radiation into a half space, $ \\Omega = 2\\pi $ sr.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = ?lspsys`
%%
%[text] ### `MicTransfer` — Transfer to the microphone
%[text] `(1,:) double` \[Pa.s/m3\]
%[text] Transfer $ H\_{mic} = p\_{mic}/U\_d $ from the diaphragm volume velocity to the pressure at the microphone, from the method `micTransfer` of the enclosure. It is empty for enclosures that do not give it, such as `comp.ClosedBox`; `comp.FeaEnclosure` gives it from the FEA model.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = ?lspsys`
%%
%[text] ### `Driver` — Driver of the system
%[text] `(1,1) comp.Driver`
%[text] Copy of the driver of the system, from `lspsys.Enclosure.Driver`. `splMax`, `splPowerLimited`, `splExcursionLimited` and `splMaxPeakVoltage` use its limits: see [`comp.Driver`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','comp','driver','driverdoc.m'))).
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = ?lspsys`
%%
%[text] ### `MicRadius` — Distance to the microphone
%[text] `(1,1) double` \[m\]
%[text] Distance $ r\_{mic} $ from the radiation surface to the microphone, at which `Pressure` and `SoundPressureLevel` are calculated. Must be positive. It can be changed after the calculation, without solving the system again. Default: 1 m.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = public`
%%
%[text] ### `ExcursionLimit` — Excursion limit for the maximum level
%[text] `(1,1) string` or `(1,1) double` \[m\]
%[text] Excursion limit that `splMax`, `splExcursionLimited` and `splMaxPeakVoltage` use: the name of an excursion limit of `Driver` (`"Xmax"`, `"Xvar"`, `"Xlim"` or `"Xmech"`), or a peak excursion in m, one way. `createResult` copies it from `Driver.ExcursionLimit`; set it to use another limit without changing the driver. Default: `"Xvar"`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = public`
%%
%[text] ### `PowerLimit` — Power limit for the maximum level
%[text] `(1,1) string` or `(1,1) double` \[W\]
%[text] Power limit that `splMax`, `splPowerLimited` and `splMaxPeakVoltage` use: the name of a power limit of `Driver` (`"Pnom"`, `"Pcont"`, `"Paes1984"` or `"Paes2012"`), or a power in W, the real power into the electrical impedance. `createResult` copies it from `Driver.PowerLimit`; set it to use another limit without changing the driver. Default: `"Pnom"`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = public`
%%
%[text] ### `ElectricalImpedance` — Electrical impedance of the system
%[text] `(1,:) double` \[Ohm\]
%[text] Complex impedance $ Z\_e $ of the whole system as the source sees it, including the motional part of the driver and the load of the enclosure:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ Z\_e = \\frac{e\_g}{i\_g} $
%[text] **Attributes:** `Dependent`
%%
%[text] ### `Pressure` — Sound pressure
%[text] `(1,:) double` \[Pa\]
%[text] When `MicTransfer` is not empty, the pressure at the microphone of the model of the enclosure (for example an FEA model) is that transfer times the diaphragm volume velocity, and `MicRadius` has no effect:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ p = H\_{mic} U\_d $
%[text] Otherwise, the pressure follows from the radiated volume velocity, as described below.
%[text] Complex sound pressure $ p $ on the axis at the distance $ r\_{mic} $, in the far field of a source with the volume velocity $ U\_{rad} $ that radiates into the solid angle $ \\Omega $ (Beranek and Mellow):
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ p = \\frac{j \\omega \\rho U\_{rad}}{\\Omega r\_{mic}} e^{-jkr\_{mic}} $
%[text] with the angular frequency $ \\omega $, the density of air $ \\rho $ and the wave number $ k $ from `lspsys`. An unsupported `RadiationAngle` gives the error `result:unsupportedRadiationAngle`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `Dependent`
%%
%[text] ### `SoundPressureLevel` — Sound pressure level
%[text] `(1,:) double` \[dB\]
%[text] Level of the RMS pressure $ p $ relative to the reference pressure $ p\_0 $ = 20e-6 Pa of `lspsys`:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\mathrm{SPL} = 20 \\log\_{10} \\frac{|p|}{p\_0} $
%[text] **Attributes:** `Dependent`
%%
%[text] ### `DiaphragmExcursion` — Excursion of the diaphragm
%[text] `(1,:) double` \[m\]
%[text] Complex excursion $ x\_d $ of the diaphragm, from its volume velocity and the effective area $ S\_d $ of `Driver`. It is an RMS value, like `SourceVoltage`; `DiaphragmPeakExcursion` gives the peak excursion of a sine signal:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ x\_d = \\frac{U\_d}{j \\omega S\_d} $
%[text] **Attributes:** `Dependent`
%%
%[text] ### `DiaphragmPeakExcursion` — Peak excursion of the diaphragm
%[text] `(1,:) double` \[m\]
%[text] Peak excursion $ \\hat{x}\_d $ of the diaphragm for a sine signal, one way, which you compare directly with $ X\_{max} $ or $ X\_{var} $:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\hat{x}\_d = \\sqrt{2} \\, |x\_d| $
%[text] **Attributes:** `Dependent`
%%
%[text] ## Methods
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[180,80,-1]}
%[text] | Method | Type | Description |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | [`splMax`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','result','methods','splmaxdoc','splmaxdoc.m'))) | Instance | Highest sound pressure level that the power and the excursion limits allow, for a sine signal: the lower of the next two. |
%[text] | [`splPowerLimited`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','result','methods','splpowerlimiteddoc','splpowerlimiteddoc.m'))) | Instance | Sound pressure level that the power limit `PowerLimit` alone allows. |
%[text] | [`splExcursionLimited`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','result','methods','splexcursionlimiteddoc','splexcursionlimiteddoc.m'))) | Instance | Sound pressure level that the excursion limit `ExcursionLimit` alone allows. |
%[text] | [`splMaxPeakVoltage`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','result','methods','splmaxpeakvoltagedoc','splmaxpeakvoltagedoc.m'))) | Instance | Peak source voltage of a sine signal at which the level reaches `splMax`. |
%[text:table]
%%
%[text] ## Examples
%%
%[text] ### Example: Sound pressure level at two distances
%[text] Create a loudspeaker system, give it a closed box of 200 liter and a driver according to the specifications of a B&C 21SW152-8, and plot the sound pressure level at 1 m. Then change `MicRadius` to 2 m: the level drops by 6 dB without solving the system again.
sys = lspsys;
sys.Enclosure = comp.ClosedBox;
sys.Enclosure.RearVolume = 200e-3;
sys.Enclosure.Driver = comp.Driver;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Re = 6;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Le = 2.2e-3;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Qes = 0.38;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Qms = 6.4;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Fs = 32;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Sd = 1680e-4;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Vas = 200e-3;
res = sys.createResult;
semilogx(res.Frequency,res.SoundPressureLevel,LineWidth=1.5,DisplayName="1 m") %[output:64f85d0a]
res.MicRadius = 2;
hold on %[output:64f85d0a]
semilogx(res.Frequency,res.SoundPressureLevel,LineWidth=1.5,DisplayName="2 m") %[output:64f85d0a]
hold off %[output:64f85d0a]
grid on %[output:64f85d0a]
xlabel("Frequency [Hz]") %[output:64f85d0a]
ylabel("Sound pressure level [dB]") %[output:64f85d0a]
legend(Location="northeast") %[output:64f85d0a]
title("Sound pressure level at two distances") %[output:64f85d0a]
%%
%[text] ### Example: Electrical impedance
%[text] Continue with the result of the previous example and plot the magnitude of the electrical impedance. The peak lies at the resonance frequency of the driver in the box.
semilogx(res.Frequency,abs(res.ElectricalImpedance),LineWidth=1.5) %[output:0db9a1c2]
grid on %[output:0db9a1c2]
xlabel("Frequency [Hz]") %[output:0db9a1c2]
ylabel("Electrical impedance [\Omega]") %[output:0db9a1c2]
title("Electrical impedance of the loudspeaker system") %[output:0db9a1c2]
%%
%[text] ### Example: Excursion of the diaphragm
%[text] Continue with the result of the first example and plot the peak excursion of the diaphragm at 2.83 V. The excursion is largest at low frequency and falls quickly above the resonance frequency of the driver in the box, where the mass of the moving system controls it.
semilogx(res.Frequency,res.DiaphragmPeakExcursion*1e3,LineWidth=1.5) %[output:4c159fa4]
grid on %[output:4c159fa4]
xlabel("Frequency [Hz]") %[output:4c159fa4]
ylabel("Peak excursion [mm]") %[output:4c159fa4]
title("Excursion of the diaphragm at 2.83 V") %[output:4c159fa4]
%%
%[text] ### Example: Maximum sound pressure level
%[text] Continue with the system of the first example. Give the driver the limits of its datasheet and plot the maximum sound pressure level at 1 m, with the levels that the power and the excursion allow alone. Below about 35 Hz the excursion ($ X\_{var} $ = 16 mm) sets the maximum, above it the nominal power (2000 W at $ Z\_{min} $ = 7 Ohm).
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Znom = 8;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Zmin = 7;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Pnom = 2000;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Xvar = 16e-3;
res = sys.createResult;
splMax = res.splMax;
powerLevel = res.splPowerLimited;
excursionLevel = res.splExcursionLimited;
semilogx(res.Frequency,splMax,res.Frequency,powerLevel,"--",res.Frequency,excursionLevel,":",LineWidth=1.5) %[output:7356fc67]
grid on %[output:7356fc67]
ylim([90 150]) %[output:7356fc67]
xlabel("Frequency [Hz]") %[output:7356fc67]
ylabel("Sound pressure level [dB]") %[output:7356fc67]
legend("Maximum","Power limit","Excursion limit",Location="northeast") %[output:7356fc67]
title("Maximum sound pressure level at 1 m") %[output:7356fc67]

